<section xml:id="sec-ch05-03-GreensTheoremCurl" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Green's Theorem: 2D Curl</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Suppose you have a SCPS curve <m>C</m> representing a boundary inside
      some liquid whose particles' motion is given by a vector field
      <m>\vec{F}</m>. Intuitively, it makes sense that how the liquid
      particles behave near the boundary <m>C</m> is influenced by what the
      particles are doing elsewhere in the region <m>R</m> bounded by
      <m>C</m>. For example, if everywhere you look inside the region
      <m>R</m>, particles are rotating counter-clockwise, it makes sense
      that this is what is happening around the boundary, as shown below:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-03-vortices">
    <caption>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, enclosing multiple small counter-clockwise vortices.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_25_1353f9c42b95b0608b3cg-1. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-03-vortices" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_11_25_1353f9c42b95b0608b3cg-1.png">
          <shortdescription>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, enclosing multiple small counter-clockwise vortices.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, enclosing multiple small counter-clockwise vortices.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We quantify the rotational motion of particles at a point using curl.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>2D Curl</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> be a
        differentiable vector field representing the motion of particles.
        We measure the rotational motion at a point <m>(a, b)</m> by using
        <md>
          \operatorname{curl} \vec{F}(a, b)=\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}(a, b)-\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(a, b)
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      When we say <q>at a point</q>, we really mean inside an infinitely
      small circle centered at <m>(a, b)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>The sign of curl <m>\vec{F}(a, b)</m></title>

    <p>
      The sign of curl <m>\vec{F}(a, b)</m> tells us the orientation of the
      rotation:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If curl <m>\vec{F}(a, b) \gt 0,(a, b)</m> has counter-clockwise
            rotational motion around it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If curl <m>\vec{F}(a, b) \lt 0,(a, b)</m> has clockwise
            rotational motion around it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If curl <m>\vec{F}(a, b)=0</m>, there is no discernible
            rotational motion around <m>(a, b)</m> and we say <m>(a, b)</m>
            is irrotational.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-03-ex-25-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the vector field
        <m>\vec{F}(x, y)=\left\langle x^2y, 9 x \right\rangle</m> :
      </p>

      <p>(a) Compute curl <m>\vec{F}</m>.</p>

      <p>
        (b) Find a point with counter-clockwise motion around it and one
        with clockwise motion.
      </p>

      <p>(c) Find all points that are irrotational.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          \operatorname{Curl}\vec F = g_x - f_y = 9-x^2
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Counter-clockwise motion implies positive curl, while clockwise
        motion implies negative curl.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\operatorname{Curl}\vec F \gt 0 \Rightarrow 9 - x^2 \gt 0 \Rightarrow 9 \gt x^2 \Rightarrow -3 \lt x \lt  3</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{An easy point satisfying this restriction is }(0,0), \ \operatorname{Curl}\vec F(0,0) = 9 \gt 0</mrow>
          <mrow>\operatorname{Curl}\vec F \lt 0 \Rightarrow 9 - x^2 \lt 0 \Rightarrow x^2 \gt 9 \Rightarrow x \lt -3 \ \text{or} \ x \gt 3</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{An easy point satisfying this restriction is }(4,0), \ \operatorname{Curl}\vec F(4,0) = 9-16 = -7 \lt 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Irrotational implies zero curl.
        <md>
          \operatorname{Curl} \vec F = 0 \Rightarrow 9- x^2 = 0 \Rightarrow x= \pm 3
        </md>
        All points of the form <m>(\pm 3 , y)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    This relationship between rotation inside a region and its boundary is
    quantified mathematically by Green's Theorem.
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <title>Green's Theorem: Curl</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>C</m> be a SCPS curve, oriented counter-clockwise, that
        encloses a connected and simply connected region <m>R</m> in the
        plane. Assume that <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m>, where
        <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> have continuous first partial derivatives in
        <m>R</m>. Then
        <md>
          \oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\iint_{R}\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right) d A  =\iint_R \operatorname{Curl} \vec F \ dA
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> and
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle</m>, the following are
      equivalent:
      <md>
        \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot d \vec{r}=\int_{C} f d x+g d y
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Since we are dealing with curl, we assume that <m>C</m> is
            simple and closed (so that it encloses a region <m>R</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Important: This theorem says that in order to compute the curl
            along a SCPS boundary, you add up the curl at each point inside
            the enclosed region <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The proof of this result is simple and is given in the book.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-03-ex-25-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using Green's theorem, compute the work represented by
        <md>
          \oint_{C} -\frac{y^3}{3} dx + \frac{x^3}{3} d y
        </md>,
        where <m>C</m> is the unit circle (oriented counter-clockwise).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From previous material <m>W = \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s</m>.
        According to the above
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\oint_{C} f d x+g d y, \  \text{for }\vec F = \langle f, g \rangle
        </md>
        This implies that in this case the vector field is
        <m>\vec F = \langle -\frac{y^3}{3}, \frac{x^3}{3} \rangle</m> whose
        <m>\operatorname{Curl} \vec F = g_x - f_y = x^2+y^2</m>.
        Additionally, from the theorem
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s= \iint_R \operatorname{Curl} \vec F \ dA
        </md>.
        Notice that both region that of integration as well as the
        function behave better in polar coordinates. In this coordinate
        system the region is expressed as <m>0\leq r \leq 1</m> and
        <m>0\leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m>, while
        <m>\operatorname{Curl} \vec F (r,\theta ) = r^2</m>. Hence
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\iint_R \operatorname{Curl} \vec F \ dA = \iint_R x^2+y^2 \ dA \overset{\text{Polar}}{=} \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^1 r^2 r\ dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^3 \ dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \left. \frac{r^4}{4} \right |_0^1 d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi } \frac{1}{4} \ d\theta = \left. \frac{\theta}{4} \right |_0^{2\pi} = \frac{\pi}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>Green's theorem can greatly simplify computations.</p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-03-ex-25-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the circulation of a fluid whose velocity field is given by
        <m>\vec{F}= \left\langle x y, x-1\right\rangle</m>, along the
        boundary given by the rectangle (oriented counter-clockwise) with
        vertices at <m>(2,-2),(3,-2),(3,2)</m>, and <m>(2,2)</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-03-ex-25-3">
        <caption>A rectangle in the plane with vertices (2,-2), (3,-2), (3,2) and (2,2), shaded as the region of integration.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 25.3. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-03-ex-25-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-25.3.png">
              <shortdescription>A rectangle in the plane with vertices (2,-2), (3,-2), (3,2) and (2,2), shaded as the region of integration.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A rectangle in the plane with vertices <m>(2,-2)</m>, <m>(3,-2)</m>, <m>(3,2)</m> and <m>(2,2)</m>, shaded as the region of integration.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Circulation <m>= \int_C \vec F \cdot \vec T \ ds</m>. Using the
        previous theorem, the integral can be expressed as
        <m>\iint_R \operatorname{Curl}\vec F \ d A</m>. For the vector
        field one has <m>f(x,y) = xy</m> and <m>g(x,y) = x-1</m>. The
        <m>\operatorname{Curl}\vec F</m> of this vector field equals
        <m>g_x-f_y= 1- x</m>. Now
        <md>
          \iint_R \operatorname{Curl}\vec F \ d A = \int_{-2}^2\int_2^3 1 -x \ dx dy = \int_{-2}^2 \left .  x - \frac{x^2}{2} \right |_2^3 dy =  \int_{-2}^2 -\frac{3}{2} \ dy = \left . -\frac{3}{2} y \right |_{-2}^2 =  - 6
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Green's Theorem and Area</title>

    <p>
      Suppose that we want to compute the curl of the vector field
      <m>\vec{F}=\langle 0, x\rangle</m> along some SCPS boundary. We have
      that
      <m>\oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s  =\oint_{C} x d y =\iint_{R}\left(\frac{\partial g}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right) d A =\iint_{R} d A</m>
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Line Integrals for Area</title>

    <p>
      To find the area of a region enclosed by a SCPS curve <m>C</m>,
      compute the line integral over <m>C</m> of
      <m>\vec{F}=\langle 0, x\rangle</m> or <m>\vec{F}=\langle-y, 0\rangle</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-03-ex-25-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a formula for the area of the ellipse
        <m>\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By utilizing the vector field <m>\vec F = \langle 0 , x \rangle</m>
        one can calculate the line integral of this vector field over a
        parametrization of the ellipse. One such parametrization is
        <m>\vec r(t) = \langle a\cos (t), b\sin (t) \rangle</m> for
        <m>0\leq t \leq 2\pi</m>. Now
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r (t)) = \langle 0 , a\cos (t) \rangle, \ \vec r'(t) = \langle -a \sin (t),  b\cos (t) \rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>\vec F (\vec r(t)) \cdot  \vec r' (t) = ab\cos^2 (t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\oint_C \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T}\ d s = \int_0^{2\pi}  \vec F (\vec r(t)) \cdot  \vec r' (t) \ dt =  \int_0^{2\pi}  ab\cos^2 (t) \ dt \overset{\text{Reduction Formula}}{=}</mrow>
          <mrow>ab \left ( \cancelto{0}{\left.  \frac{1}{2}\cos (t) \sin (t) \right |_0^{2\pi}} +\frac{1}{2}\int_0^{2\pi} \cos^0 (t) \ dt \right ) = ab\left ( \frac{1}{2} \int_0^{2\pi} dt \right ) = \left . ab\frac{1}{2} t\right |_0^{2\pi} = ab\pi</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      If a vector field <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m> is conservative
      on an open, connected, simply connected region <m>R</m>, then
      <m>\operatorname{curl} \vec{F}=g_{x}-f_{y}=0</m> for every point in
      <m>R</m> (hence <m>\vec{F}</m> is irrotational in <m>R</m> ).
      Conversely, if <m>\vec{F}</m> is irrotational in <m>R</m>, its curl
      along the boundary of <m>R</m> is zero (by Green's theorem). Hence,
      we have the following equivalence.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Conservative and Irrotational Fields</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be an open, connected, simply connected region. Then
      <m>\vec{F}</m> is conservative on <m>R</m> if and only if it is
      irrotational in <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

</section>
